<CompareLayout
	slug="agentic-workflow-platforms"
	h1="Rocketflare vs Dust, Copilot Studio, n8n and Mastra"
	lede="There are two common ways to put AI agents to work in a business. You can rent them in someone else’s workspace, or assemble them from a framework, a workflow tool, a vector store, an identity provider and a front end, each with its own bill. Rocketflare is a third way: the whole agentic business app, agents included, as one codebase you own and deploy as a single unit."
	glance={[
		{ label: 'Agent platforms give you', body: 'Agents for your staff, in the vendor’s cloud, priced per seat, credit or execution.' },
		{ label: 'Frameworks give you', body: 'Excellent agent building blocks, and the rest of the application left to build.' },
		{ label: 'Rocketflare gives you', body: 'Agents, durable workflows with approvals, a knowledge base, the app, its users and roles, and a record of what happened. One repository, one deploy, MIT licensed.' },
	]}
	shortAnswer="If the agents are for your own staff and you need them this month, Dust, Copilot Studio or n8n will serve you well. If the agents are part of something you sell, or you need to own where the data lives and what it costs, start from Rocketflare: a self-contained agentic business app with durable agents, human approval, a knowledge base, tenancy and cost tracking, deployed as one Cloudflare Worker over your own Postgres."
	profiles={profiles}
	table={table}
	differences={differences}
	pickThem={pickThem}
	faqs={faqs}
	sources={sources}
>
	<Fragment slot="intro">
		<section class="define">
			<div class="wrap">
				<SectionHead
					eyebrow="The term"
					title="What we mean by an agentic business app"
					lede="An application where AI agents carry out steps of a business process inside the same app that holds the users, their permissions, the data and the record of what happened. Four properties set it apart from an agent platform."
				/>
				<ul class="props">
					{properties.map((p) => (
						<li>
							<span class="chip tone-purple"><ConceptIcon name={p.icon} /></span>
							<h3>{p.title}</h3>
							<p>{p.body}</p>
						</li>
					))}
				</ul>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="sprawl">
			<div class="wrap sprawl-grid">
				<div class="sprawl-copy">
					<span class="eyebrow">Self-contained</span>
					<h2>One unit you own, or a stack nobody does</h2>
					<p>
						The usual production agent setup is a set of services joined by API keys: an agent
						platform, a workflow tool, a vector store holding a second copy of your documents, an
						identity provider, a front end someone still has to build, and a tracing vendor. Each has
						its own admin screen, its own bill and its own idea of who your users are. When something
						goes wrong, the answer is spread across all of them.
					</p>
					<p>
						A Rocketflare app holds the same parts in one repository. The agents read the same tables
						as the screens, obey the same roles, write to the same activity log and are released
						with the same tag. You can reason about it, test it, and hand it to someone else.
					</p>
				</div>
				<figure class="sprawl-figure">
					<DiagramSprawl parts={sprawlParts} layers={sprawlLayers} describedBy="sprawl-table" footer="one repo · one Worker · your Postgres" />
